# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chonkboard — what must never be committed, and why.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build output. The binary is reproducible from source; the Tailwind CLI is a
# ~120MB platform binary fetched by `make tools`.
/bin/chonkboard
/bin/tailwindcss
/bin/
/tmp/
/dist/
chonkboard
build-errors.log

# Generated code and assets. Both are reproducible: *_templ.go from the .templ
# sources, app.css from input.css. Committing them invites a merge conflict in a
# file nobody edits, and a stale copy that silently ships.
*_templ.go
*_templ.txt
/web/static/css/app.css

# Live data. Everything the app owns lives in one directory; none of it belongs
# in version control. The -wal and -shm files are WAL sidecars — committing a
# database without them is committing a torn snapshot.
/data/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-journal

# Backups are snapshots of production data.
/backups/
*.db.bak

# Secrets and local configuration. .env.example is the committed template.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!*.pub

# Test and coverage artefacts
coverage.out
coverage.html
*.test
*.out
/testdata/tmp/

# Go tooling. Anchored: an unanchored "vendor/" also swallows
# web/static/vendor/, which holds the pinned front-end libraries that must be
# committed — that is the entire point of vendoring them.
/vendor/
go.work
go.work.sum

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Node is deliberately absent from this project — Tailwind runs from the
# standalone CLI. These entries exist so an accidental `npm init` does not get
# committed before someone notices.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock

# Local agent scratch. .project-doc/ IS committed — it is the project's record.
.claude/settings.local.json
